In this lesson we'll look at parabolas, those familiar U shaped curves you might recognise from maths class. The simplest one is y equals x squared, which sits with its lowest point, called the vertex, right at the origin. From there we can build more interesting parabolas using a general form where the vertex can be shifted anywhere on the plane. The letter a is the key player here. If a is positive, the parabola opens upwards like a smile. If a is negative, it flips and opens downwards like a frown. The size of a also matters. A bigger a makes the parabola narrower and steeper, while a smaller a makes it wider and flatter, stretching out the arms. When you read a parabola's graph, you can spot the vertex as the turning point, the axis of symmetry as a vertical line straight through it, and the intercepts where it crosses the axes. Now let's see it in action.